# LIBRARY CAPABILITY MATRIX — Zero Ambient Authority rollup

File: openOODA/audit/library_capability_matrix.oot
Source-text isolation of `ooda/std`. Not `oodac check`. Not emit+gcc GREEN.
Counts come from `scripts/audit_library_capabilities.oo` walking live `.oo` files with `read_file`.

## 1. Scope

The crawler opens every `.oo` file under `ooda/std`.
It skips `.bin` leftovers and `.c` emit dumps.
It uses one detector for synthetic bodies and for std modules.

A sealed effect call needs a matching `&Cap` name in that same file.
The detector treats an op as a call only when a non-identifier character sits before the name and `(` follows (spaces allowed).
Fragments such as `prefetch`, `usb_cdc_fetch_rx`, `max_tcp_connections`, comment `No fetch`, and a field `alloc_bytes` are not calls.

The op list is `process_exec`, `sys_exec`, `sys_spawn`, `read_file`, `write_file`, `fetch`, `tcp_connect`, `alloc_bytes`, and `landlock_restrict`.
Matching tokens follow SECURITY.oot: `&ProcessCap` or `&SysCap`, `&FsReadCap` or `&FsCap`, `&FsWriteCap` or `&FsCap`, `&HttpCap` or `&NetCap`, `&TcpCap` or `&NetCap`, `&AllocCap`, `&SysCap`.

This is token-vs-call text. It is not capability arg-flow.

## 2. Synthetic lock

Ambient body: `process_exec(`, `read_file(`, and `fetch(` with no `&Cap`.
The detector must report a violation.

Capped body: the same calls with `&ProcessCap`, `&FsReadCap`, and `&HttpCap`.
The detector must stay clean.

Fragment body: `No fetch`, `usb_cdc_fetch_rx`, `prefetch`, `max_tcp_connections`, and `alloc_bytes: Int`.
The detector must stay clean.

Comment body: `process_exec(` with `&ProcessCap` plus a `fetch` word that is not a call.
The detector must stay clean.

